om  these communities really showing just  how powerhouse diverse scientists can be  remember to always push it away from you  providing opportunities for both current  students and potential future ones  ashley field global news halifax  ahead an emotional drink the long road a  bc first nations community took to  finally tap in to a clean water supply  well after decades of waiting some  residents of the cowichan tribes the  largest single first nation band in  british columbia finally have clean  drinking water  members of the first nation on vancouver  island have been living there since the  1940s but as kylie stanton reports the  community has never had potable water  despite being just two kilometers from  the nearest city  as water pours from this tap it's almost  hard to believe have a drink baby for  residents living on 20 properties along  indian road in cowichan tribes this is a  first it's been  a long time coming that's for sure yeah  it was unbelievable for me i got pretty  choked up about it it's just like wow i  can't believe it we're here  we can drink water from our own tap baba  koshimit has relied on either well or  bottled water his entire life more than  three decades of it spent in cowichan  raising his family what he says had its  challenges you couldn't even consider  drinking it was fizzing like pop when it  came out of the top want another drink  his daughter has since been raising her  children under the same conditions the  water only suitable for laundry and  bathing on good days having to use  bottled water for everything from  cooking dinner to  like bathing our babies to you know  making a jug of juice had to come out of  a bottle it's definitely a whole new  world for us  something so simple so many of us take  for granted is something to celebrate  here  [Applause]  the community marking the milestone with  a ribbon-cutting ceremony on friday you  know when we think about  water that's not safe for our community  to drink we think of this happening in  rural communities but really it's here  in we're you know the biggest nation  here in bc and we don't have clean  drinking water for our people the homes  are located here neighboring the city of  duncan just two kilometers away but it's  taken decades of work to finally land  the necessary federal funding to make  clean drinking water a reality i think  that it's really good for people to be  aware of and not take advantage of  things that they do have the luxuries  that they do have  there is still work to be done roughly  another 20 homes have yet to secure  access and there's currently no timeline  as to when that's expected to happen my  heart goes out to those that are still  having to live that way because i know  what that was like so still there are no  grudges here  only gratitude forever grateful kylie  stanton global news  next conquering the rubik's cube how  these calgary siblings are taking their  skills to a global stage  [Applause]  nasam  brought hockey's biggest prize back to  his hometown of london ontario today  celebrating with family friends and  close-knit fans qadri is believed to be  the first muslim player to become a  stanley cup champion now before kicking  off a parade he took the cup to the  london muslim mosque which is minutes  away from where four members of the  ufsell family were killed and another  boy was injured in a hate crime in june  last year and is the same mosque the  family attended  you know these are the streets i used to  run around as a kid  and uh you know to be to bring this big  shiny trophy back to my hometown it's  really come full circle  and i just thank you guys for being here  with me  [Applause]  it is believed to be the first time the  stanley cup has ever been inside a  mosque in its  124 year history  cadre who began his career with toronto  has signed with the calgary flames after  winning the cup with the colorado  avalanche  he says he wants the moment to act as  inspiration and motivation for the next  generation of canadians  now to the canadian siblings who have  managed to conquer one of the world's  best known puzzles eric ismail who is  nine can solve the rubik's cube in just  12 seconds his sister zora born with one  hand can do it in about a minute and a  half  our jamie dahl spoke with the calgary  kids who first picked up the cube during  the pandemic and to their father a  doctor treating alzheimer's about the  cube's benefits beyond the fun  seven years old in the middle of a  pandemic eric ismail picked up his first  rubik's cube he borrowed one from oh  from the neighbor he borrowed one from a  neighbor  it was two-sided i learned how to solve  that  easy-peasy  it was really stiff and hard but it just  reminded me of  you know over 30 years ago when i did a  little bit of cubing  my dad told me that when he was little  he could solve the big ones like these  with three  before long he was faster than me and  then twice as fast and then three times  as fast and then you were left in the  dust  in the dust now nine he's been to two  competitions one in sweden and one in  edmonton he studies videos and  researches algorithms constantly trying  to get faster my record is  12.94 so that's 12 seconds  everywhere he goes he brings a cube  i always i'm always like i can solve it  too  eric's sister zora can solve it in under  a minute and a half  born with just one hand but she doesn't  let that get in her way of anything i  ride horses and i do gymnastics i'm a  normal human i can do  what everyone else can do if not better  i don't usually say this but  i in my mind i think that's actually  really cool how she can solve it with  one hand now she's striving to catch up  to her little brother's cube record  definitely to be faster when they have  their cubes and it draws a bit of a  crowd they feel proud of themselves and  each other  it's more than just a fun hobby when  he's not cubing with his kids dr zane  ismail is an alzheimer specialist  frankly the best prevention starts  at this age when you build cognitive  reserve and brain reserve  with cubing with cubing with you know  enriched activities with sports with  bilingualism eric now has his sights set  on making it to the finals in  competition and the siblings are already  teaching their youngest brother how to  solve puzzles  jamie daw global news  wow that is so impressive and that is  global national for this saturday night  i'm tracy tong tonight's your canada is  this field of sunflowers in dougald  manitoba we love seeing your canada so  please keep emailing your photos to  viewers at globalnational.com  thanks for watching see you again  tomorrow have a great night  [Music]
 

